  "worked_solution": "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=4.$ Substituting $y(1)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n4={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=-10.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =-6{c}_{1}{x}^{-7}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-7}-6{c}_{2}{x}^{-7}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=-10$ and ${c}_{1}=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =-6(4)+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =4{x}^{-6}+14{x}^{-6}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\  & =\\frac{4+14\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
